          "content": "# Unit 1 capstone \u2014 Digitizer chain and sampling specification\n\n## Engineering problem\n\nSelect a bandlimited 0\u20132 kHz synthetic sensor input, sample it at a declared rate, add a controlled out-of-band interferer and quantify analog filtering needed to avoid aliasing. The capstone intentionally uses invented signals, not field measurements. The unit provides the mathematical concepts: Discrete-frequency notation and periodicity, Sampling and analog anti-alias filtering, Finite precision quantization and clipping, Discrete convolution and impulse tests, DSP requirements and validation signals. Write a concise problem statement and choose consistent notation for time, sample index, physical units and the transform convention. Keep raw input parameters in a table separate from derived outputs.\n\n## Required submissions\n\n1. A mathematical model listing all assumptions, independent variables and initial conditions or finite record lengths.\n2. A hand-verifiable result with the steps from governing equation to value or functional expression.\n3. One independent computation, using a different method from the hand calculation, with an explicit discrepancy tolerance.\n4. A plot that labels units, sample rate, window and phase/frequency conventions when relevant.\n5. Two stress tests: one boundary case and one deliberate model violation with the expected failure symptoms.\n6. A short engineering report distinguishing verification of code from validation against physical data.\n\n## Evaluation rubric (100 points)\n\n- Mathematical and physical specification: 20.\n- Correct derivations and units: 25.\n- Independently reproducible numerical result: 20.\n- Boundary and violation tests: 20.\n- Clear interpretation and data provenance: 15.\n\n## Discussion prompts\n\nExplain why your assumptions are necessary; demonstrate whether changing the record length or excitation changes the result; discuss which error is numerical and which is modeling error; and propose a minimal real-world test to determine whether the simplified model is appropriate. A visually convincing trace without a numeric reference earns no numerical verification credit. Refer to the corresponding lessons for the domain-specific limitations.\n"
